      "text": "Let's denote Jane's current age as \\( x \\). According to the problem, one year ago, her age was \\( x - 1 \\), and one year from now, her age will be \\( x + 1 \\). We are given that one year ago her age was a perfect square, and one year from now her age will be a perfect cube.\n\nFirst, we express the conditions mathematically:\n1. One year ago, Jane's age was a perfect square:\n   \\[\n   x - 1 = k^2 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k.\n   \\]\n2. One year from now, Jane's age will be a perfect cube:\n   \\[\n   x + 1 = m^3 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m.\n   \\]\n\nWe can rewrite the equations as:\n\\[\nx = k^2 + 1\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nx = m^3 - 1.\n\\]\nBy setting the two expressions for \\( x \\) equal to each other, we get:\n\\[\nk^2 + 1 = m^3 - 1.\n\\]\nRearranging the terms, we obtain:\n\\[\nk^2 = m^3 - 2.\n\\]\nWe need to find integer solutions to this equation. Let's test some small values for \\( m \\):\n\n- If \\( m = 2 \\):\n  \\[\n  k^2 = 2^3 - 2 = 8 - 2 = 6.\n  \\]\n  Since 6 is not a perfect square, \\( m = 2 \\) is not a solution.\n\n- If \\( m = 3 \\):\n  \\[\n  k^2 = 3^3 - 2 = 27 - 2 = 25.\n  \\]\n  Since 25 is a perfect square (\\( 5^2 = 25 \\)), \\( k = 5 \\) is a solution. Therefore, \\( x = k^2 + 1 = 5^2 + 1 = 25 + 1 = 26 \\).\n\nTo verify, we check the conditions:\n- One year ago, Jane's age was \\( 26 - 1 = 25 \\), which is a perfect square (\\( 5^2 = 25 \\)).\n- One year from now, Jane's age will be \\( 26 + 1 = 27 \\), which is a perfect cube (\\( 3^3 = 27 \\)).\n\nThus, Jane is \\(\\boxed{26}\\) years old.",
