      "text": "Let \\( J \\) denote Jane's current age. According to the problem, one year ago, Jane's age was a perfect square. This can be expressed as:\n\\[\nJ - 1 = k^2\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nOne year from now, Jane's age will be a perfect cube. This can be expressed as:\n\\[\nJ + 1 = m^3\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\).\n\nWe now have the following system of equations:\n\\[\nJ - 1 = k^2\n\\]\n\\[\nJ + 1 = m^3\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we can solve for \\( J \\):\n\\[\nJ = k^2 + 1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( J = k^2 + 1 \\) into the second equation:\n\\[\nk^2 + 1 + 1 = m^3\n\\]\n\\[\nk^2 + 2 = m^3\n\\]\n\nWe need to find values of \\( k \\) and \\( m \\) such that \\( k^2 + 2 = m^3 \\). We will test small integer values for \\( k \\) to find a suitable \\( m \\).\n\n1. If \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[\nk^2 + 2 = 1^2 + 2 = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad m^3 = 3 \\quad \\text{(not a perfect cube)}\n\\]\n\n2. If \\( k = 2 \\):\n\\[\nk^2 + 2 = 2^2 + 2 = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad m^3 = 4 \\quad \\text{(not a perfect cube)}\n\\]\n\n3. If \\( k = 3 \\):\n\\[\nk^2 + 2 = 3^2 + 2 = 11 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad m^3 = 11 \\quad \\text{(not a perfect cube)}\n\\]\n\n4. If \\( k = 4 \\):\n\\[\nk^2 + 2 = 4^2 + 2 = 18 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad m^3 = 18 \\quad \\text{(not a perfect cube)}\n\\]\n\n5. If \\( k = 5 \\):\n\\[\nk^2 + 2 = 5^2 + 2 = 27 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad m^3 = 27 \\quad \\text{(which is a perfect cube)}\n\\]\nSince \\( 27 = 3^3 \\), \\( m = 3 \\).\n\nIf \\( k = 5 \\), then:\n\\[\nJ = k^2 + 1 = 5^2 + 1 = 25 + 1 = 26\n\\]\n\nThus, Jane's current age is \\(\\boxed{26}\\).\n\nTo verify, one year ago Jane was \\(25\\) and \\(26\\), and one year from now, Jane will be \\(27\\), which is \\(3^3\\). The conditions of the problem are satisfied, confirming that Jane's current age is indeed \\(\\boxed{26}\\).",
